\mt1 Psalms

\c 7

\d A turbulent song of David. He sang it before the LORD when Cush, of the tribe of Benjamin, came after him.\f + \fr 7:0 \fk KONTEXT \fq A turbulent song \ft The Hebrew word is *shiggāyôn* — a musical or genre term that appears only here and in Habakkuk 3:1 (plural: *shigionoth*). Its meaning was already lost by the time the ancient Greek translation (LXX) was made; the translators simply transliterated the term rather than translate it. The probable root *šāgāh* means "to wander" or "to err," suggesting a composition that moves restlessly through emotional registers — desperation, formal solemnity, fierce urgency, settled trust, dark wisdom, anticipatory praise, all in seventeen verses. The Akkadian cognate *šigû* means "lamentation." The descriptor "turbulent song" attempts to honor the word's emotional-restlessness signal without pretending we know exactly what musical form it indexed. — The superscription has practical force: it tells the reader what kind of attention to bring. This is not a calm psalm. The voice will jump registers. The disorientation is part of what the psalm is doing.\f*\f + \fr 7:0 \fk KULTUR \fq Cush, of the tribe of Benjamin \ft Cush appears nowhere else in the Hebrew Bible. Medieval Jewish tradition — most notably Rashi — identifies Cush with Saul: "just as a Cushite is distinctive in appearance, Saul was distinctive in his deeds." It is an ironic euphemism: Saul, David's first persecutor, named obliquely as the figure behind the prayer. The Greek tradition (LXX) connects the name to Chusi from the Absalom revolt (2 Samuel 15–17). Modern scholars (Yitzhak Berger, *Expository Times* 2014) argue for a real but lost historical figure in the David–Saul conflict. — The detail that matters: Benjamin was Saul's tribe. To name Cush as a Benjaminite is to mark the adversary as part of the king's faction — political persecution from the dominant tribal structure. This is not a private quarrel. This is a hunted person with a powerful network arrayed against them. — One footnote of accountability: Augustine's *Expositions of the Psalms* converted "Cush the Benjaminite" into a Christological allegory (Cush = "silence" = mystery of the Passion; Jemini = "right-handed" = Resurrection), and from there into a typology of Jewish opposition to Christ. This reading has done documented harm — it erased the psalm's specific political content (David hunted by Saul's faction) and made the psalm a vehicle for Christian anti-Judaism. Rashi's reading restores what Augustine's allegory erased. We follow Rashi here. The psalm is for hunted people. It works because it is specific.\f*

\s1 Hunted

\b
\q1
\v 1 LORD — my God —\f + \fr 7:1 \fk KULTUR \fq I have run to you \ft The Hebrew is *ḥāsîtî* — a perfect-tense verb from *ḥāsâ*, "to take refuge, to seek asylum." This is not the language of casual emotional trust. It is the language of *physical* refuge — running to a sanctuary, a designated place of legal protection. In ancient Near Eastern law, an accused person could flee to the holy place and claim the right to have their case heard before God rather than before a corrupt court. Solomon's prayer in 1 Kings 8:31–32 describes the procedure: the falsely accused stands at the altar, swears their innocence, and waits for divine verdict. — The perfect tense matters. The psalmist does not say "I will run to you" or "I am running to you." She says "I have already run to you." The case is already filed. The asylum has already been claimed. What follows is not the request; it is the proceedings.\f*
\q1 I have run to you.

\b
\q1 Save me from everyone hunting me.
\q1 Pull me out.

\b
\q1
\v 2 Don't let him tear my throat out — lion-quick —\f + \fr 7:2 \fk TEXT \fq tear my throat out \ft Most English Bibles render the Hebrew *napšî* as "my soul." This is technically defensible but culturally misleading. *Nepeš* in biblical Hebrew is not the Platonic immortal soul. It is the throat — the passage through which breath and food enter — and by extension the whole breathing, hungry, frightened, embodied self. When the psalmist says "tear out my *napšî* like a lion," she means: tear my throat out. The image is bodily, not spiritual. Anyone who has felt their pulse spike at a real physical threat recognizes the register: the breath catches in the throat first; the whole body knows the danger through that one part. — The shift to the singular "him" (after the plural "everyone hunting me") is a Hebrew poetic move: out of the pack, one figure stands forward — the leader, the named accuser, the one whose teeth would actually close on the throat. Translations that smooth out the singular-plural shift lose the impassioned realism of the original.\f*\f + \fr 7:2 \fk LEBEN \fq with no one to step in \ft The Hebrew is *ʾên maṣṣîl* — "no one rescuing." It is not a description of personal loneliness; it is a description of *structural* powerlessness. The psalm is composed for people whose social location offers no human advocate: the foreigner without standing, the woman without family backing, the poor without resources, the slave without rights. — Anyone who has tried to bring a complaint against a powerful institution recognizes the experience the phrase names. You make the call; no one calls back. You file the complaint; it goes nowhere. Friends who would normally help calculate the cost of intervention and find it too high. The silence is not accidental — it is the way power works when the powerful are protecting their own. — This is the psalm's social location. The prayer exists because the human courts have failed. Communities that have known this experience — enslaved Africans in the American South praying the Psalter, prisoners under apartheid, Christians and Jews under authoritarian regimes — have always known what this verse means. For readers shaped by relatively functional institutions: the verse names the limits case. What do you pray when the system itself is the predator?\f*
\q1 rip me apart with no one to step in.

\s1 I Swear

\b
\q1
\v 3 LORD, my God —
\q1 if I have done this thing,
\q1 if there is wrongdoing on my hands,\f + \fr 7:3 \fk KULTUR \fq if I have done this thing \ft Verses 3–5 are an *oath of clearance* — a formal legal procedure attested across the ancient Near East. The structure is deliberate: three conditional "if" clauses describing the alleged wrongdoing, followed by a triple consequence — what should happen to the speaker if the accusation is true. Hittite vassal treaties, Assyrian loyalty oaths, and biblical legal texts (Numbers 5:11–28; Deuteronomy 19; 1 Kings 8:31–32) all employ this structure. — The point is not bravado. The point is the opposite of bravado. The oath of clearance is the most serious self-binding act available in ancient juridical procedure. The accused stakes their own destruction on the truth of their testimony. If they are lying, they invite the consequence themselves — they have signed off on it. — The modern equivalent is placing one's hand on a Bible in a courtroom and swearing to tell the truth. The conditional self-curse formalizes the speaker's submission to the highest possible scrutiny. The psalmist is not being reckless; she is doing the most formal thing a person can do.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 4 if I have paid back evil for good,
\q1 if I have oppressed my enemy for no reason —\f + \fr 7:4 \fk TEXT \fq if I have oppressed my enemy for no reason \ft This verse contains the most significant textual difficulty in the psalm. The Masoretic Text reads a form that appears to mean "if I have *delivered* my enemy without cause" — which the NICOT commentary, citing Jeffrey Tigay, calls "a monstrosity: it makes rescuing one's enemy a sin." Near-universal scholarly consensus reverses the first two letters of the Hebrew verb (*ḥālaṣ* → *lāḥaṣ*), yielding "if I have *oppressed* my foe without cause." The corrected reading is adopted by NICOT (deClaissé-Walford, Jacobson, Tanner), Goldingay, Craigie, and confirmed independently by the liberation theologian Elsa Tamez. Aperto follows this corrected reading. — The theological weight of the correction is enormous. The MT reading would have the psalmist defending herself by saying "I have not done good to my enemy without cause" — making *not* helping enemies a form of innocence. The corrected reading has the psalmist defending herself by saying "I have not been an oppressor" — placing the boundary of righteousness at *not exploiting power over others*. Tamez identifies this as the structural ethic of the psalm: God sides with those who are not the *lāḥaṣ* oppressor. This is the version of the verse that the rest of the psalm requires.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 5 let him hunt me down. Let him catch me.
\q1 Let him grind my life into the ground
\q1 and drag my name through the dust.\f + \fr 7:5 \fk KULTUR \fq drag my name through the dust \ft The Hebrew is *ûkĕbôdî leʿāpār yaškēn* — "and let my honor settle into the dust." The word *kābôd* — usually rendered "honor" or "glory" — does not mean ego or vanity. It means *social existence*: the standing one has in the community, the weight one carries in collective life, the right to be heard. To have one's *kābôd* laid in the dust is to be unmade as a person who counts. — In honor-shame cultures (which include the ancient Near East and remain dominant in much of the contemporary world), the destruction of one's *kābôd* is functionally equivalent to social death. After such a destruction, you can still walk around — but no one greets you, no one trades with you, no one's daughter marries your son. The community treats you as if you no longer exist. — The psalmist is staking *this* — her social existence, her ability to be a person who counts in her community — on the truth of her oath. If she is lying, she invites total social death. The oath is not "may I die" (though physical destruction is also implied in v. 5a). It is "may I become nothing in the eyes of everyone who knew me." For ancient hearers and for many readers today, that is the deeper threat.\f*

\b
\q1 \qac \qac*\qa Selah\qac*\qac*

\s1 Rise Up

\b
\q1
\v 6 Rise up, LORD — in your anger.\f + \fr 7:6 \fk KONTEXT \fq Rise up... Stand tall... Wake \ft The triple imperative — *qûmāh / hinnāśēʾ / ʿûrāh* — is not generic religious enthusiasm. It is a direct echo of Numbers 10:35, the ritual invocation spoken when the Ark of the Covenant was lifted to lead Israel into battle: "Rise up, O LORD! Let your enemies be scattered!" This is the prayer the king prayed before going to war — the formal summons to the Divine Warrior. — In Psalm 7, that prayer is on the lips of a single accused individual. J. Clinton McCann (*The Shape and Shaping of the Psalter*, 1993) calls this a "democratization" of the royal warrior motif: the cosmic power that the king invoked for national struggles is now invoked by a private person facing false accusation. The pastoral implication is enormous. The voiceless individual, with no human advocate, has the same access to the divine court that the king has. Whatever cosmic resources are available to royal power are also available to the falsely accused. — The triple imperative also models a kind of insistence in prayer: not a polite request, but the explicit summoning of God's anger as a *resource* for the innocent. This is bold prayer. It presupposes that God's anger is available — not only as a threat to fear, but as a force to be invoked on behalf of those who cannot defend themselves.\f*
\q1 Stand tall against the fury of those who hate me.
\q1 Wake — for me.
\q1 You commanded justice.\f + \fr 7:6 \fk TEXT \fq You commanded justice \ft The Hebrew is *ṣiwwîtā mišpāṭ* — literally, "you have commanded *mišpāṭ*." The word *mišpāṭ* is broader than its English translations suggest: it means judgment in the legal sense, but also the verdict, the ruling, the legal-ethical order itself. To say "you commanded *mišpāṭ*" is to say that God has spoken justice into the world's order. Alter: "You ordained justice." The verse is the ground of the appeal — the psalmist is reminding God that the legal-ethical order of creation is God's own design, and asking God to act consistent with that design. — This is a recognizable Hebrew prayer move: arguing not from human merit but from God's own character or commitment. It is the same logic that drives the appeal to *ḥesed* (covenant loyalty) elsewhere in the Psalter: God set this up; God is bound to maintain it. The psalmist is not begging for special favor. She is asking the system's designer to run the system.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 7 All the nations gather around you.
\q1 Take the high seat.

\b
\q1
\v 8 LORD — judge them all.
\q1 Judge me by my righteousness,\f + \fr 7:8 \fk TEXT \fq Judge me by my righteousness \ft Modern English-speaking readers — especially those shaped by Christian piety — often hear this phrase as inappropriate self-righteousness. "Who prays 'judge me according to my righteousness'?" the secular reader asks. "Isn't this just spiritual boasting?" The hearing is wrong. — The psalmist is not claiming sinless perfection. She is claiming *situational* innocence — innocent of *this specific accusation*. NICOT (citing James L. Mays): "The psalms know that there is no autonomous independent righteousness on the basis of which human beings can deal with God (Pss 130:3; 143:2). Such prayers were composed for a person who was in the right *in comparison with an antagonist*." Job makes the identical move in Job 31. The protestation of innocence is a *legal* category, not a *theological* one. The speaker is saying: I am not the person they say I am; I did not do the thing they say I did. The verdict should reflect the facts, not the accuser's claim. — The phrase has been weaponized historically — used to support claims of elect-nation status, to sanctify colonial violence, to construct out-groups as "the wicked." Each of these misuses depends on collapsing situational righteousness into absolute righteousness. The psalm itself contains the corrective: in vv. 9 and 11, the same God who is asked to judge the psalmist is named as "examiner of hearts and conscience." The psalmist invites scrutiny of her own heart as the *condition* for asking divine intervention. This is not a claim of exemption from examination; it is submission to the most thorough possible examination. Anyone who reads the psalm as authorizing their own innocence without that submission has misread its formal structure.\f*
\q1 by my integrity.

\b
\q1
\v 9 Let the wickedness of the wicked end.
\q1 Make the righteous stand.
\q1 You who test hearts and conscience —\f + \fr 7:9 \fk KULTUR \fq hearts and conscience \ft The Hebrew is *libbôt ûkĕlāyôt* — "hearts and kidneys." In Hebrew anthropology, the kidneys (*kĕlāyôt*) were understood as the seat of conscience: the deepest moral interior, the part of the self that no one else can see. Psalm 16:7 has the speaker "instructed by my kidneys in the night"; Psalm 26:2 asks God to "test my kidneys and my heart"; Jeremiah 11:20 and 17:10 use the same idiom for divine moral examination. — Modern English translations typically render this "minds and hearts" or, following Robert Alter, "hearts and conscience." The translation here keeps Alter's "conscience" because it preserves the moral-forensic register the kidney-idiom carried for ancient hearers. — The picture is precise. God is performing what amounts to a forensic examination of the inside of a person — reading the hidden moral interior that no human witness can see. This is what makes the divine court incorruptible: human courts can only judge external evidence, but the divine judge sees the *kĕlāyôt*, the inside no testimony can reach. For someone falsely accused, this is good news of the highest order. The case will not be decided by which side has the better lawyer or the more credible witnesses. It will be decided by the one who can read the inside.\f*
\q1 just God.

\s1 My Shield

\b
\q1
\v 10 My shield is God —\f + \fr 7:10 \fk KONTEXT \fq My shield is God \ft The Hebrew word is *māgēn*, "shield" — the most ordinary military object in the ancient world, carried by every soldier. After the Divine Warrior summoned in v. 6, with anger and the rising imperative, the shift to *shield* in v. 10 is theologically significant. The same God who is summoned to *attack* in v. 6 is named as *cover* in v. 10. The Warrior is also the protector. — Goldingay names this the modulation that holds the psalm's military imagery together. Without v. 10's shift to shield, the divine action of the psalm would be only aggressive. With it, the divine action is also defensive — the same energy that goes out to confront wickedness also covers the vulnerable. For traditions that have inherited only the warrior side of the imagery (and used it to sanctify their own violence), v. 10 is a corrective. The full picture is: God who fights *for* the innocent, God who covers the innocent. Take the shield away and the warrior becomes a tyrant; take the warrior away and the shield is just decoration.\f*
\q1 who saves anyone whose heart is straight.

\b
\q1
\v 11 God is a just judge.
\q1 God passes sentence every single day.\f + \fr 7:11 \fk TEXT \fq passes sentence every single day \ft The Hebrew *zōʿēm bĕkol-yôm* literally means something like "indignant every day" or "passes judgment every day." NICOT and Hossfeld-Zenger emphasize that this is a *daily* judicial activity — not eschatological, not reserved for some final day, but ongoing as God's ordinary governance of the world. The Hebrew Bible's sense of justice is woven into the daily fabric of creation, not deferred to a later cosmic verdict. — The translation "passes sentence every single day" makes the daily-ness audible and keeps the legal-forensic register. Some versions soften this — the Orthodox Study Bible reads "patient judge, not bringing down wrath every single day" — but this softening reflects later theological discomfort, not the plain sense of the Hebrew. The pastoral force of the verse for the falsely accused: God is not a delayed verdict. God is a present, daily court. The case is already being heard.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 12 If a person doesn't turn back —\f + \fr 7:12 \fk UNGLAUBLICH \fq God sharpens his sword. Bends his bow. Strings it. \ft Modern readers often stumble on the divine warrior imagery. Does God really sharpen swords and string bows? Is this a violent tribal deity, anthropomorphized into something embarrassing? — Three frames help here. (1) Critical/skeptical reading: the imagery is mythological residue from Israel's Canaanite cultural matrix; it should be read as poetic metaphor at most, and probably as a primitive theological substrate the tradition has worked to outgrow. (2) Symbolic/theological reading: the weapons are poetic figures for the active force of justice. Theodoret of Cyr, writing in the fifth century, observed that the verse describes God's *threat* of action, not literal divine armament: God who has the means to act, not God who acts violently in fact. (3) Historical/believing reading: the imagery insists, against all temptations to soft-pedal divine justice, that God's verdict has *consequences*. A judge who only declares verdicts but never carries them out is not actually a judge. — Whichever frame the reader inhabits, the rhetorical force is the same. The wicked are not given a soft pass. The system has teeth. Whether this reads as comforting or as troubling depends on whether you are the falsely accused or the false accuser. The psalm — which Aperto reads with the falsely accused — frames active divine justice as the only hope of those who have no other recourse. The corrupt court will not act. The lying witness will not be exposed by his peers. If God's justice is only declarative and never active, the innocent never see vindication. The verse refuses to leave the innocent without recourse. — On the subject of the verbs: there is a long-standing scholarly question about whether God or the enemy is the subject of the sharpening, bending, and stringing. Aperto follows NICOT and Hossfeld-Zenger: the subject is God throughout. The conditional "if a person does not turn back" refers to the wicked one's refusal to repent; the consequence-clauses describe God's action. This reading integrates the shield/judge metaphor of vv. 10–11 with the active-justice metaphor of vv. 12–13: the same God shields the innocent and acts against the wicked who refuse to step back from the line.\f*
\q1 God sharpens his sword.
\q1 Bends his bow. Strings it.

\b
\q1
\v 13 He prepares the weapons of death,
\q1 his arrows tipped with fire.

\s1 The Pit He Was Digging

\b
\q1
\v 14 \add Look —\add* he labors with sin.\f + \fr 7:14 \fk KULTUR \fq he labors with sin... gives birth to lies \ft The Hebrew uses three verbs from the biological vocabulary of pregnancy and birth: *yĕḥabbel* (writhes in labor), *hārâ* (conceives), *yālad* (gives birth). They are not decorative metaphors. They are pointed satire. The wicked man is described with the language usually reserved for a mother in childbirth — and what he produces, with all that labor, is *deceit*. — Robert Alter, in his commentary, calls this image "satiric and gendered": the application of pregnancy verbs to a male villain is the ironic heart of the verse. The body that should be productive of life is shown to be productive only of harm. Modern readers may find the imagery strange or jarring; ancient hearers would have heard the satire immediately. — The structural theology behind the verse is what scholars call act-consequence theology, or the "boomerang principle." The Hebrew Bible's wisdom literature (Proverbs, Ecclesiastes, Job) repeatedly observes that wickedness contains within itself the mechanism of its own undoing. The wicked do not need to be punished from outside; they punish themselves through what they produce. The verse describes the moral universe as it actually works — slow, sometimes; partial, often — but eventually, what the wicked carry inside them comes out, and what comes out destroys them.\f*
\q1 He has conceived trouble.
\q1 He gives birth to lies.

\b
\q1
\v 15 He dug a pit. He hollowed it deep.
\q1 And he fell —
\q1 into the very pit he had been digging.\f + \fr 7:15 \fk KONTEXT \fq into the very pit he had been digging \ft The image is one of the most universal in ancient wisdom literature. Proverbs 26:27 gives it canonically: "Whoever digs a pit will fall into it, and a stone will come back on the one who starts it rolling." Sirach 27:26 (in the Apocrypha): "Whoever digs a pit will fall into it, and whoever sets a snare will be caught by it." Mesopotamian proverb collections preserve close parallels. The image was a cross-cultural commonplace. — The deepest biblical instance of the structure is the story of Esther: Haman builds a gallows fifty cubits high to hang Mordecai, and is himself hanged on it (Esther 7:10). Jewish tradition recites Psalm 7 at Purim precisely because the structure of the psalm — false accusation by a Benjaminite adversary, the wicked falling into the trap they set — maps exactly onto the Esther story. (Mordecai = innocent psalmist; Haman = the one who falls into his own pit.) For Jewish readers, the psalm has always been heard as the theology of Purim: the survival of a people through the self-defeat of those who tried to destroy them. — One translation note: the Hebrew uses the imperfect aspect for the digging verb in the second clause — *yipʿāl*, "he was making/working." The wicked man is *still digging* when he falls in. The comic timing is built into the grammar.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 16 His own trouble comes back on his head.
\q1 His own violence falls on his face.\f + \fr 7:16 \fk LEBEN \fq His own trouble comes back on his head \ft The closing observation of the wisdom section reframes the question of vengeance. The psalmist does not pick up a weapon. The psalmist does not plot retaliation. The wicked are not destroyed by the psalmist's action — they are destroyed by their own. — Erich Zenger (*A God of Vengeance?*, 1996) argues that this is what makes the imprecatory dimensions of the Psalter ethically defensible: the violence is placed in God's hands, not the speaker's, and the operative mechanism is the wicked one's own behavior. The psalmist's role is to name what is happening, not to make it happen. — For readers shaped by the modern therapeutic vocabulary of "letting go" or "releasing the need for vindication," the verse offers an older formulation of the same insight. You do not have to pursue revenge. The structure of the moral universe will do its work without you. Your task is to step out of the equation and let the consequence find its target. — One pastoral caution. The verse does not promise that the wicked always fall, or that justice always arrives in this life. The Psalter contains other psalms (most notably Psalm 73) that explicitly grapple with the times when the wicked appear to prosper and the innocent suffer. The boomerang theology is a *trust* about the direction of history, not a formula that has never failed. It says: this is how the moral universe is built. It does not say: this is how every individual case will resolve.\f*

\s1 Most High

\b
\q1
\v 17 I will praise the LORD for his justice.\f + \fr 7:17 \fk LEBEN \fq I will praise the LORD for his justice \ft The closing verse is, in formal terms, a *todah* — a thanksgiving vow. The psalm has been a lament; it ends as anticipated thanksgiving. This is the genre pivot, and it is the psalm's most distinctive spiritual move. — The vindication has not arrived. The case is still pending. The lion is still in the field. And yet, praise. The verb form is cohortative — *ʾôdeh*, "let me praise" or "I will praise" — a committed future, not a present state. The speaker is not claiming that everything is now fine. She is *deciding* to face toward vindication before any evidence of it. This is not denial; it is orientation. — Note what the praise is *for*. Not for the rescue (which has not arrived). Not for the verdict (which is still pending). The praise is *for his justice* — *kĕṣidqô* — for God's *character*. The shift is from circumstance to character: the psalmist will not wait for circumstantial vindication to begin praising the character that produces vindications. — For contemporary spiritual practice — across traditions and including secular psychological frames — this is the move worth carrying out of the psalm. You do not have to wait for resolution to begin orienting toward it. You can decide, before the evidence, to face toward what you trust the universe is moving toward. Whether you call that God or the long arc of justice or the structure of the moral universe, the psychological and spiritual mechanism is the same. The psalm names it with exceptional precision. — The final word — *ʿElyôn*, "Most High" — pulls the camera all the way back. The single accused person, alone with the high judge, looking up. That is where the psalm ends.\f*
\q1 I will sing the name of the LORD —
\q1 Most High.
